<!--
  ats_invalid_impossible_date.xml — DESPITE THE FILE NAME, THIS DOCUMENT IS
  ACCEPTED. The file name records the intent of the case, not its verdict.

  Rule exercised: fechaType, ats.xsd:16-24. Its only facet is the pattern at
  ats.xsd:22-23,
    "(0[1-9]|[12][0-9]|3[01])[/](0[1-9]|1[012])[/](19|20)\d\d"
  which bounds the day to 01-31 and the month to 01-12 but never relates them
  to the calendar. 31/02/2020 — February has no 31st — matches the pattern and
  therefore validates.

  Element under test: detalleCompras/fechaEmision, ats.xsd:738.
  The same applies to fechaRegistro (ats.xsd:733) and to the whole fechaType
  family used across every block, including the deprecated fechaEmiRet1 /
  fechaEmiRet2 and the fechaPagoDiv split-payment date.

  THIS IS WHERE THE SCHEMA IS NOT THE NORM: the XSD is a lexical shape, not a
  calendar. A builder that hands the XSD an unvalidated string can produce a
  document that passes every grammar check and is still rejected at SRI
  reception. Date validity is one of the checks the business validators must
  own.
-->
